</code></pre><p>So now we just need to show the arctans all collapse to $\pi$. Recall the identity</p>
<p>$$
\tan^{-1} a \pm \tan^{-1} b = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{a\pm b}{1\mp ab}\right).
$$</p>
<p>The sum of the first four terms can be calculated easily in Common Lisp:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>% sbcl --no-inform
* (defun combine (a b) (/ (+ a b) (- 1 (* a b))))
COMBINE
* (reduce #'combine '(3/2 -1/2 11/24 19/48))
4
</code></pre><p>So we have $2\big(\tan^{-1}4 + \tan^{-1}(1/4)\big)$, and with our final elementary trig
identity $\tan^{-1} (a/b) = \pi/2 - \tan^{-1} (b/a)$, we find $S = \pi$.</p>
<h2 id="a-new-discovery">A new discovery?</h2>
<p>Of course, I was excited to find this formula, but after some internet spelunking, it turns out
it had already been discovered by <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20181225155904if_/http://gery.huvent.pagesperso-orange.fr:80/index_explorer_net.htm">GÃ©ry Huvent</a>
and <a href="http://www.pi314.net/eng/hypergse6.php">Boris GourÃ©vitch</a>, perhaps independently. GourÃ©vitch
doesn&rsquo;t credit Huvent as he does with other formulas, but he does say
&ldquo;[&hellip;] furthermore, we can obtain BBP formula [&hellip;] by using what Gery Huvent calls
the denomination tables [&hellip;].&rdquo; Daisuke Takahashi cites Huvent&rsquo;s website in
<a href="https://tsukuba.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/2001720/files/RJ_51-1-177.pdf">this 2019 paper</a> published in
<em>The Ramanujan Journal</em>. In all cases, they write the formula in the following way:</p>
<p>$$
\frac{1}{128} \sum _{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^{12k}}\left(
\frac{768}{24 k+3}+\frac{512}{24k+4}+\frac{128}{24 k+6}-\frac{16}{24 k+12}-\frac{16}{24 k+14}-\frac{12}{24
k+15}+\frac{2}{24 k+20}-\frac{1}{24 k+22}\right),
$$</p>
<p>which is structurally equivalent to $S$.</p>
<p>Despite having been known already, this formula doesn&rsquo;t appear to be <em>well</em> known. As such, I
hope this blog post brings more attention to it.</p>
